{"units": [{"id": "aēsyros-word2", "translation": "light, nimble"}, {"id": "aēsyros-word3", "translation": "ἀείσυροι [swift, light] M ε"}, {"id": "aētos-orth1", "translation": "great, mighty"}, {"id": "aētos-def1", "translation": "great, mighty"}, {"id": "aētos-note1", "translation": "Hesychius: ἀήτους [great, mighty]: μεγάλας [great]. It is a Homeric word, who said θάρσος ἄητον [great courage] and other things, on which see Thes. vol. 1 p. 803."}, {"id": "aētos-note2", "translation": "[ζηται. See ἡμαι.]"}, {"id": "aētos-word1", "translation": "ἀήτους [great, mighty]: μεγάλας [great]"}, {"id": "aētos-word2", "translation": "θάρσος ἄητον [great courage]"}, {"id": "aētos-word3", "translation": "ζηται [to be sought]"}, {"id": "aētos-word4", "translation": "ἡμαι [I sit]"}, {"id": "athamantis-orth1", "translation": "daughter of Athamas"}, {"id": "athamantis-def1", "translation": "daughter of Athamas"}, {"id": "athamantis-quote1", "translation": "Helle, daughter of Athamas."}, {"id": "athamas-orth1", "translation": "Athamas"}, {"id": "athamas-note1", "translation": "was the title of a play of Aeschylus (fr. 1-4b)."}, {"id": "athana-orth1", "translation": "Athena"}, {"id": "athana-def1", "translation": "Athena"}, {"id": "athana-note1", "translation": "The tragedians constantly use the Doric form Αθάνα [Athena] for Ἀθηνᾶ [Athena] in every kind of meter, but they keep the n in the form Αθηναία [Athena], which will be discussed below."}, {"id": "athana-quote1", "translation": "Queen Athena."}, {"id": "athana-quote2", "translation": "Onca Athena."}, {"id": "athana-word1", "translation": "Αθάνα [Athena]"}, {"id": "athana-word2", "translation": "Ἀθηνᾶ [Athena]"}, {"id": "athana-word3", "translation": "Αθηναία [Athena]"}, {"id": "athanai-orth1", "translation": "Athens"}, {"id": "athanai-note1", "translation": "Doric for Ἀθῆναι [Athens], which see."}, {"id": "athanai-word1", "translation": "Ἀθῆναι [Athens]"}, {"id": "athanatos-orth1", "translation": "immortal"}, {"id": "athanatos-def1", "translation": "immortal"}, {"id": "athanatos-def2", "translation": "conferring immortality"}, {"id": "athanatos-note1", "translation": "(the first syllable is always long)"}, {"id": "athanatos-note2", "translation": "He could have written ἀθανάτου [immortal], but following Homer's example he preferred the feminine ending."}, {"id": "athanatos-quote1", "translation": "to keep one's hands off the immortals."}, {"id": "athanatos-quote2", "translation": "among the immortals and those beneath the earth."}, {"id": "athanatos-quote3", "translation": "the end of the immortals."}, {"id": "athanatos-quote4", "translation": "immortal skin."}, {"id": "athanatos-quote5", "translation": "having robbed Nisus of his immortal lock."}, {"id": "athanatos-word1", "translation": "ἀθανάτου [immortal]"}, {"id": "athaptos-orth1", "translation": "unburied"}, {"id": "athaptos-def1", "translation": "unburied"}, {"id": "athaptos-quote1", "translation": "to cast out the corpse of Polyneices unburied."}, {"id": "athaptos-quote2", "translation": "But this man will not be unburied."}, {"id": "atheleos-orth1", "translation": "unwilling"}, {"id": "atheleos-def1", "translation": "unwilling"}, {"id": "atheleos-note1", "translation": "Thus Homer Il. 4, 43 said ἑκὼν ἀέκοντί γε θυμῷ [willing, yet with unwilling heart]."}, {"id": "atheleos-quote1", "translation": "willing, unwilling."}, {"id": "atheleos-word1", "translation": "ἑκὼν ἀέκοντί γε θυμῷ [willing, yet with unwilling heart]"}, {"id": "athelktos-orth1", "translation": "not to be soothed, harsh, implacable"}, {"id": "athelktos-def1", "translation": "not to be soothed, harsh, implacable"}, {"id": "athelktos-quote1", "translation": "But you might charm one who is not to be charmed."}, {"id": "atheos-orth1", "translation": "impious, godless"}, {"id": "atheos-def1", "translation": "impious, godless"}, {"id": "atheos-quote1", "translation": "the penalty of insolence and godless thoughts."}, {"id": "atheos-quote2", "translation": "a godless man and bitter to his parents."}, {"id": "atheos-quote3", "translation": "with godless foot."}, {"id": "atheristos-orth1", "translation": "unreaped"}, {"id": "atheristos-def1", "translation": "unreaped"}, {"id": "atheristos-note1", "translation": "(in Bekk. Anecd. p. 353, 10) where see the note."}, {"id": "atheristos-quote1", "translation": "bronze unreaped (thus Blomfield in Praef. Agam. p. X for ἀθέριτον), towering over shields (the codex has ἀσπίδος),"}, {"id": "athermantos-orth1", "translation": "not heated. Figuratively, not bold"}, {"id": "athermantos-def1", "translation": "not heated. Figuratively, not bold"}, {"id": "athermantos-note1", "translation": "Schol. ἀθράσυντον [not bold]: for the Lemnian women are celebrated for their boldness."}, {"id": "athermantos-quote1", "translation": "honoring the unheated hearth of the house, the womanly, timid spear."}, {"id": "athermantos-word1", "translation": "ἀθράσυντον [not bold]: for the Lemnian women are celebrated for their boldness"}, {"id": "athetōs-orth1", "translation": "lawlessly, arbitrarily"}, {"id": "athetōs-def1", "translation": "lawlessly, arbitrarily"}, {"id": "athetōs-note1", "translation": "αθέσμως [lawlessly] M from a gloss. Emended from Hesychius: ἀθέτως [lawlessly]: ἀθέσμως [lawlessly], οὐ συγκατατεθειμένως [not in accordance with established custom]. Aeschylus in Prometheus Bound."}, {"id": "athetōs-quote1", "translation": "Zeus rules lawlessly."}, {"id": "athetōs-word1", "translation": "αθέσμως [lawlessly] M"}, {"id": "athetōs-word2", "translation": "ἀθέτως [lawlessly]: ἀθέσμως [lawlessly], οὐ συγκατατεθειμένως [not in accordance with established custom]. Aeschylus in Prometheus Bound"}, {"id": "athēnai-orth1", "translation": "Athens"}, {"id": "athēnai-def1", "translation": "Athens"}, {"id": "athēnai-def2", "translation": "Athens, a city of Euboea"}, {"id": "athēnai-note1", "translation": "Doric form Ἀθῆναι [Athens] in lyric passages"}, {"id": "athēnai-note2", "translation": "where see the note."}, {"id": "athēnai-quote1", "translation": "Where do they say Athens is situated in the land?"}, {"id": "athēnai-quote2", "translation": "How I groan when I remember Athens."}, {"id": "athēnai-quote3", "translation": "Is the city of Athens still unsacked?"}, {"id": "athēnai-quote4", "translation": "he found vengeance for glorious Athens."}, {"id": "athēnai-quote5", "translation": "the whole army has been destroyed around Athens."}, {"id": "athēnai-quote6", "translation": "Remember Athens."}, {"id": "athēnai-quote7", "translation": "And hateful Athens with torches."}, {"id": "athēnai-quote8", "translation": "having seen ancient, hateful Athens."}, {"id": "athēnai-quote9", "translation": "passing by Athens Diadas,"}, {"id": "athēnai-word1", "translation": "Ἀθῆναι [Athens]"}, {"id": "athēnaia-orth1", "translation": "Athena"}, {"id": "athēnaia-def1", "translation": "Athena"}, {"id": "athēnaia-note1", "translation": "It is an old Attic form of the name, frequent in inscriptions,"}, {"id": "athēnaia-quote1", "translation": "the queen of this land, Athena."}, {"id": "athēnaia-quote2", "translation": "the might of Athena."}, {"id": "athēnaia-quote3", "translation": "the great ordinance of Athena."}, {"id": "athēnaios-orth1", "translation": "Athenian"}, {"id": "athēnaios-def1", "translation": "Athenian"}, {"id": "athēnaios-quote1", "translation": "from the Athenian army."}, {"id": "athēr-orth1", "translation": "spike, ear of corn"}, {"id": "athēr-def1", "translation": "spike, ear of corn"}]}